title: 'eth-rpc: remove blocks orphaned by a reorg while the server was stopped'
doc:
- audience: Node Dev
  description: |-
    An archive eth-rpc that was stopped while one of its indexed best blocks was replaced by a
    reorg kept serving that block after the restart. `eth_getTransactionReceipt`,
    `eth_getTransactionByHash` and `eth_getLogs` returned the orphaned transaction and its logs,
    while `eth_getBlockByNumber` returned the canonical block at the same height.

    Indexed blocks now record their block number. On startup the fork detection cache is filled
    from the latest indexed blocks, so a new block at one of those heights replaces the old one as
    it would without a restart. Indexing a finalized block, from the finalized block subscription
    or the historic sync, also removes every other block indexed at that height.

    The database gains a `block_number` column on `eth_to_substrate_blocks`, added by a migration
    that runs on startup. Blocks indexed before the upgrade have no block number and are not
    cleaned up by this change. A database that already holds orphaned blocks from an earlier
    reorg keeps them; delete it and resync to remove them. After the migration has run, older
    eth-rpc versions refuse to open the database.

    `ReceiptProvider::remove_non_canonical_blocks` is new. No configuration change is needed.
crates:
- name: pallet-revive-eth-rpc
  bump: minor
